WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.399
I am in Iceland. It is both stunningly
beautiful and

00:00:03.399 --> 00:00:07.149
stunningly cold, and it's also a very
good place to talk about something

00:00:07.149 --> 00:00:10.240
you might not know, which is names.
Specifically,

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:13.580
how a lot of the world's names—perhaps
even a majority—

00:00:13.580 --> 00:00:17.539
don't fit into the English system of
names given to you by your parents

00:00:17.539 --> 00:00:21.560
and then names inherited from your parents. Here in Iceland,

00:00:21.560 --> 00:00:24.859
for example, the idea that you have a family name handed down

00:00:24.859 --> 00:00:28.420
generation to generation to generation
just doesn't happen.

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.470
It's rare at best. Instead, children

00:00:31.470 --> 00:00:35.700
take their parents' first name with "son"
or "daughter" added to it.

00:00:35.700 --> 00:00:40.100
Which instantly makes the Icelandic 
telephone directory a little bit complicated: you have to

00:00:40.100 --> 00:00:43.719
index by first name and by occupation
because there are

00:00:43.719 --> 00:00:48.120
so few last names in total. And if that sounds strange, well think of all the

00:00:48.120 --> 00:00:50.010
people you know whose last names are gonna be

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.309
Thomson or Davidson or Peterson or Jameson.

00:00:53.309 --> 00:00:57.800
All of those are that Icelandic system,
which used to be the English system,

00:00:57.800 --> 00:01:01.910
it's just changed now so it is a family
name. And beyond that you have

00:01:01.910 --> 00:01:06.420
all the people who don't go by their
legal name. Technically even I don't—my legal name is

00:01:06.430 --> 00:01:07.410
Thomas Scott,

00:01:07.460 --> 00:01:11.640
but I go by Tom. Add to that the
people who go by their middle name,

00:01:11.650 --> 00:01:15.460
or who go by a nickname, or who just go by a
different name for

00:01:15.460 --> 00:01:19.390
any number of reasons. Then you've got
the cultures who put their family name

00:01:19.390 --> 00:01:22.610
first, which is always confusing when
they're trying to translate

00:01:22.610 --> 00:01:26.200
over to English. Which brings me to a
bit of advice, really.

00:01:26.240 --> 00:01:30.500
If you're asking for
people's names, either on a paper form or online,

00:01:30.530 --> 00:01:34.320
don't put first name and last name. Don't put—the worst-case—

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.579
Christian name and surname. Just give
someone a box

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:40.720
to type into—just ask them what their name is.

00:01:40.720 --> 00:01:45.299
And for bonus points, then ask them how
they want to be addressed,

00:01:45.299 --> 00:01:51.300
because, well, if you want to make friends, the
best way is to get someone's name right.

00:01:52.400 --> 00:01:54.400
[Translating these subtitles? Add your name here!]

